ble to  tell because no matter how great the  approximation is these paths all have  length 2 which is way longer than root 2  so this way they'll end up walking so  much extra distance than if they were  actually on the diagonal see that's the  bit that really messes with my head  another weird bit is that in some ways  the sequence is a good approximation  for the line if you know calculus or if  you've seen my last episode you know  that if what we cared about was the area  underneath the diagonal instead of the  diagonals length this staircase shape  has area that gets closer and closer to  the area we want so we have the sequence  of shapes that visually approximate the  line very well and we know it  approximates the area very well too and  yet it's terrible at approximating the  lines lengths this is a really worrying  example how do we know in advance when  an approximation is not going to be good  enough I really don't know so even  though we all use approximations a lot  without a second thought  even approximations that look like  they're really good can go completely  wrong well there are lots of places we  can go from here because this whole  thing brings up heaps of questions one  bit I'm really interested in is the fact  that the shape we were talking about is  actually a fractal fractals in general  are kind of the arch-nemesis of calculus  so we might take up the story from there  next time see you then
 

